ou could select cells that are  infected and uninfected and once you set  this up for the cells that you're going  to be doing you can actually save this  as a protocol to be used every time  you're going to run the assay and making  the process automated and not requiring  any dyes  thank you the next question is for David  what what is the specificity sensitivity  on these instruments yes thank you for  the question so the specificity for the  octet instruments or the octet  biosensors who depend on what you  analyze you know your biological  molecule of analysis you know is so for  example if your target analysis is an  IgG such as we have talked about this  webinar then your balance would  potentially have a protein a or protein  g you know on it the biosensors that we  currently sell used recombinant protein  a recombinant protein G where the  potential BAC or albumin binding  epitopes have been removed so there  should be specific to any FC region of  an IgG or potentially you know fusion  proteins you know so the systems are  generally specific but just like with  any given biological assay you know  sometimes one may need to as I develop  to remove potential nonspecific you know  binding when it comes to sensitivity all  the octet instruments that I talked  about today should be able to measure as  low as zero point zero five micrograms  per ml in a direct assay so a direct our  circuit is one where you are dipping the  sensors into the reagent in a one-step  assay however if you have concentrations  lower than 0.05 microns per ml then you  can build the a say in what we call an  indirect assay such as a sandwich Eliza  assay where you amplify signals using  polymerization chemistry's in that case  you can lower the sensitivity work to as  low as 10 to around 4 ml when doing a  quantitation assay  thank you the next question is for David  does the octet quantitation approach  apply only to IgG samples if not what  might the process look like for a  recombinant protein just good question  on thanks again  so IgG quantitation is what we did in  this webinar however the systems can be  used for any type of quantitation so  long as you have a capture molecule and  by that really what I mean is that if  you had a recombinant protein for  example you could potentially immobilize  a specific antibody towards that  particular protein and use that nearly  customized bio sensor to be able to do  direct quantitation either so it's not  quite the case that it's only for a GDQ  mutation it can also be used for  recombinant proteins but in that case  you would have to develop what we call  customized biosensors those customers  biosensors once developed installed  could then be used in direct acid where  they're being dipped in simply into the  analyte for one step assay  thank you the next question is for Mike  can automation be used to optimize  culture growth conditions of bacterial  expression systems for example ecoli for  the parameters such as temperature time  of harvest  shaking speed and OD yeah that's a great  question it's a similar application in  the sense of you might be looking for  something similar for protein production  but very different in the sense of now  dealing with prokaryotic cells from a  liquid handling perspective there's  they're very similar right you're still  moving things from one position to  another  but one consideration might be a lot of  times people working in bacteria are  more used to say round based place such  as petri dishes ideally you can do  things in an SBS format so basically  standard microtiter plate formats  perhaps using deep well placed to get  some of your liquid culture or as I  mentioned before the span eight  pipetting system does work well with  tubes so if you're doing things in a  shaking tube type of platform that would  also work as far as changing temperature  that's one thing to consider when it  comes to what you're integrating or what  some devices you might include obviously  if you're working with multiple samples  in one plate that plate can only be  incubated one temperature  but you could have smaller incubators or  even some on Dec heating and cooling  since you wouldn't necessarily need the  co2 typically associated with mammalian  cell culture and so we could do some of  that incubation and shaking on Dec or  through smaller integrated incubators as  well so long answer to a short question  as yes you definitely can optimize that  type of workflow with bacteria and using  a lot of the same devices heating and  cooling you can measure your OD with a  plate reader or other such analyzers so  definitely would be feasible great thank  you so much the next question is for  Mike were these experiments performed  with adherent or suspension cells yeah  that's also a great question  so cho cells are obviously able to be  grown typically under either conditions  suspension or adherence and what these I  would kind of call these loosely  adherent what we found is that after  multiple passages in the optic show the  cells even if we started them in  adherent culture would eventually switch  to suspension however we were doing this  in the first one or two passages in this  media so they were still adherent to the  bottom of the plate we have been doing  some similar work for cell line  development working with suspension show  cells and depending on the plates you  use the workflow is very similar but if  you want to take an imaging component it  does help to work with some plates like  the co-star I'll forget what they're  called but it's basically a plate that  loosely binds some suspension cells but  they can be easily released from the  plate just through pipetting so again  the system should work with either it's  just you need to take a couple things  into consideration depending on the  analysis that you're using but the octet  would be unaffected or should be  unaffected by the presence of cells so  if some do get transferred it should not  affect your your reads and again the  imaging there's ways that you can have  the cells settle to the bottom of the  plate and stay loosely adherent for more  accurate imaging thank you the next  question is for Mike wanted to know if  it was possible to build or or Beckman  has a custom integration for the octet  or are potentially working on one for  this system yes we do so then  the image that I showed the obviously  very difficult to say in a small image  but the cell line development system  that we demonstrated did have an  integrated octet and we've demonstrated  that in the past it is able to be  directly controlled through the  automation where like I said a robotic  handler would place the plate in the  octet for the read and basically  essentially push the Go button the data  would then be sent back to the  automation and as I mentioned that data  could be directly used to drive hit  picking and other applications on the  instrument so yes the octet HDX and as  David mentioned I believe that 384 have  both been integrated with biomech liquid  handlers thank you the next question is  for either David or Mike was there any  analysis of how unattached culture would  affect the cells yes I can start with  that one I think we sort of touched on  that earlier as I mentioned what we did  was using it here in cultures but again  they the cells can be grown in both  suspension or in adherent cultures as I  mentioned some of the work we're doing  now is with suspension cultures I've not  done a direct comparison we've actually  changed to a different media we're using  cho growth a for molecular devices for  the suspension cultures and the cells  are still expressing IgG but I've not  done a direct comparison to how we did  this this type of analysis but the same  same rules would apply you would  certainly be able to do a very similar  approach for optimization and then just  plate the suspension cells on top and  still process them using the octet David  anything bad no I think you're correct I  don't think I have anything to add to  that great thank you so much the next  question is for either of you it is the  effect stronger if you see glutimax plus  the spite and buffer then just glutimax  I noticed there was a difference from  the baseline but was not clear on this  difference I think so I think there was  a fairly slight difference that might  you can chime in here but what I did  that indeed when you had glutimax plus  pi and the bioreactor buffer you see a  fairly slight improvement from when he  just had both of us alone the question  in my mind is whether the difference is  significant enough to justify the cost  of adding spite in the bioreactor buffer  as opposed to just having glutamic by  itself you know so that's an analysis  that I'm not sure we've done Mike but  there indeed was a very slight  difference between the three combined  versus just glutimax alone yeah David is  exactly right so I pulled up slide 13  which hopefully can roughly demonstrate  that the green line and the third graph  is higher than the red line in the first  graph so there was an improvement with  the spy plus bioreactor buffer according  to our statistical analysis as well as  our actual readings but David is correct  that you would have to do the kind of  cost-benefit analysis of is this worth  the additional cost of the reagents and  and we didn't necessarily look at that  but but yes there was an improvement  with all of the factors that as we  mentioned the predicted best condition  would be glutimax plus polyamine plus by  plus bioreactor buffer but whether you  can get most of the way there with just  glutimax certainly seems to be the case  so again to do the kind of cost-benefit  analysis of what the reagents cost would  definitely be worthwhile looking in  thank you we'll wrap up with one last  question and that question is for Mike  which software tool was used to design  your do e and generate biom